#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:2
msgid "Configuring CLI arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:4
msgid ""
"By default extra arguments passed to the poe CLI following the task name are "
"appended to the end of a :doc:`cmd task<../tasks/task_types/cmd>`, or "
"exposed as ``sys.argv`` in a :doc:`script task<../tasks/task_types/script>`, "
"but will cause an error for other task types. Alternatively it is possible "
"to define named arguments that a task should accept, which will be "
"documented in the help for that task, and exposed to the task in a way that "
"makes the most sense for that task type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:6
msgid "In general named arguments can take one of the following three forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:8
msgid ""
"**positional arguments** which are provided directly following the name of "
"the task like"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:9
msgid ":bash:`poe task-name arg-value`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:11
msgid "**option arguments** which consist of a key and one or more values like"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:12
msgid ":bash:`poe task-name --option-name arg-value`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:14
msgid ""
"**flags** which are either provided or not, but don't accept a value like"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:15
msgid ":bash:`poe task-name --flag`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:17
msgid ""
"The value for the named argument is then accessible by name within the task "
"content, also in most cases as an environment variable, and sometimes also "
"as a native variable depending on the task type as detailed below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:21
msgid ""
"You can indicate that a variable should be private, and not propagated to "
"the task subprocess for use at runtime by naming it in lowercase with a "
"prefix of ``_``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:23
msgid ""
"Private variables can be used via parameter expansion in :doc:`cmd tasks<../"
"tasks/task_types/cmd>`, as variables in :doc:`expr tasks<../tasks/task_types/"
"expr>` or :doc:`script tasks<../tasks/task_types/script>`, but not in :doc:"
"`shell tasks<../tasks/task_types/shell>` tasks which can only evaluate "
"variables from the environment at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:27
msgid "Configuration syntax"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:29
msgid ""
"Named arguments are configured by declaring the ``args`` task option as "
"either an array or a sub-table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:33
msgid "Abbreviated form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:35
msgid ""
"The array form may contain string items which are interpreted as an option "
"argument with the given name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:37
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"script = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"args   = [\"host\", \"port\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:43 ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:59
msgid "This example can be invoked as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:45
msgid "poe serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8001"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:49
msgid ""
"When used with a :doc:`script task<../tasks/task_types/script>` in this way "
"the ``run`` function from the ``myapp`` module will be called passing the "
"``host`` and ``port`` as keyword arguments with whatever values are provided "
"on the CLI, in addition to ``host`` and ``port`` being set as environment "
"variables on the task subprocess, and the ``sys.argv`` being populated with "
"the full invocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:51
msgid ""
"The following example demonstrates a slightly different form where the "
"private ``_food`` arg is explicitly passed to the ``frying_pan`` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:53
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.cook]\n"
"script = \"kitchen:frying_pan(_food)\"\n"
"args   = [\"_food\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:61
msgid "poe cook --food eggs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:65
msgid ""
"Note that the leading underscore is stripped from the inferred CLI option. "
"The same stripping applies to positional args: a private positional arg "
"called ``_food`` is displayed as ``food`` in help while still being treated "
"as a private variable inside the task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:69
msgid "Array of inline tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:71
msgid ""
"Items in the array can also be inline tables to allow for more configuration "
"to be provided to the task like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:73
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"args = [\n"
"  { name = \"host\", default = \"localhost\" },\n"
"  { name = \"port\", default = \"9000\" }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:84
msgid "Array of tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:86
msgid ""
"If you want to provide more configuration per argument then the following "
"toml syntax can be used to declare an array of tables with each key on a "
"separate line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:88
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"help = \"Run the application server\"\n"
"\n"
"  [[tool.poe.tasks.serve.args]]\n"
"  name = \"host\"\n"
"  options = [\"-h\", \"--host\"]\n"
"  help = \"The host on which to expose the service\"\n"
"  default = \"localhost\"\n"
"\n"
"  [[tool.poe.tasks.serve.args]]\n"
"  name = \"port\"\n"
"  options = [\"-p\", \"--port\"]\n"
"  help = \"The port on which to expose the service\"\n"
"  default = \"8000\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:108
msgid "The double square brackets are toml syntax for a table within an array."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:111
msgid "Subtable configuration syntax"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:113
msgid ""
"The following toml syntax structure achieves exactly the same result as the "
"previous example but instead of ``args`` being an array of tables, it is a "
"table of tables like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:115
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"help = \"Run the application server\"\n"
"\n"
"  [tool.poe.tasks.serve.args.host]\n"
"  options = [\"-h\", \"--host\"]\n"
"  help = \"The host on which to expose the service\"\n"
"  default = \"localhost\"\n"
"\n"
"  [tool.poe.tasks.serve.args.port]\n"
"  options = [\"-p\", \"--port\"]\n"
"  help = \"The port on which to expose the service\"\n"
"  default = \"8000\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:131
msgid ""
"When using this form the ``name`` option is no longer applicable because the "
"key for the argument within the args table is taken as the name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:134
msgid "Task argument options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:136
msgid "Named arguments support the following configuration options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:138
msgid "**default** : ``str`` | ``int`` | ``float`` | ``bool``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:137
msgid "str | int | float | bool"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:139
msgid ""
"The value to use if the argument is not provided. This option has no "
"significance if the ``required`` option is set to true."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:141
msgid ""
"For string values, environment variables can be referenced using the usual "
"templating syntax as in the following example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:143
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.deploy]\n"
"cmd = \"...\"\n"
"env.AWS_REGION.default = \"eu-central-1\"\n"
"\n"
"[[tool.poe.tasks.deploy.args]]\n"
"name    = \"region\"\n"
"help    = \"The region to deploy to\"\n"
"default = \"${AWS_REGION}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:154
msgid ""
"As in the above example, this can be combined with setting an :doc:`env "
"value<../tasks/options>` on the task with the ``default`` specifier to get "
"the following precedence of values for the arg:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:156
msgid "the value passed on the command line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:157
msgid "the value of the variable set on the environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:158
msgid "the default value for the environment variable configured on the task"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:160
msgid "**help** : ``str``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:159 ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:162
msgid "str"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:161
msgid ""
"A short description of the argument to include in the documentation of the "
"task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:163
msgid "**name** : ``str``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:164
msgid "The name of the argument. Only applicable when *args* is an array."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:166
msgid "**options** : ``list[str]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:165
msgid "list[str]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:167
msgid ""
"A list of options to accept for this argument, similar to `argparse name or "
"flags <https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html#name-or-flags>`_. If "
"not provided then the name of the argument is used. You can use this option "
"to expose a different name to the CLI vs the name that is used inside the "
"task, or to specify long and short forms of the CLI option, e.g. ``[\"-h\", "
"\"--help\"]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:169
msgid "**positional** : ``bool``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:168 ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:199
msgid "bool"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:170
msgid ""
"If set to true then the argument becomes a positional argument instead of an "
"option argument. Note that positional arguments may not have ``type = "
"\"boolean\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:172
msgid "**multiple** : ``bool`` | ``int``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:171
msgid "bool | int"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:173
msgid ""
"If the ``multiple`` option is set to true on a positional or option argument "
"then that argument will accept multiple values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:175
msgid ""
"If set to a number, then the argument will accept *exactly* that number of "
"values in total."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:177
msgid ""
"For positional arguments, only the last positional argument may have the "
"``multiple`` option set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:179
msgid ""
"This option is not compatible with arguments with ``type = \"boolean\"`` "
"since these are interpreted as flags. However multiple ones or zeros can be "
"passed to an argument of type \"integer\" for similar effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:181
msgid ""
"For option arguments (flags), multiple values may be supplied in any of the "
"following styles, which can also be freely mixed in a single invocation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:183
msgid "**Space-separated** after a single flag: ``--engines v2 v8``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:184
msgid ""
"**Repeated flag**, each with its own value(s): ``--engines v2 --engines v8``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:185
msgid "**Mixed**: ``--engines v2 v8 --engines v10``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:187
msgid ""
"When ``multiple = N`` (an exact count), the *total* number of values across "
"all occurrences must equal N — e.g. with ``multiple = 2`` both ``--widgets a "
"b`` and ``--widgets a --widgets b`` are valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:189
msgid ""
"The values provided to an argument with the ``multiple`` option set are "
"available on the environment as a string of whitespace separated values. For "
"script tasks, the values will be provided to your Python function as a list "
"of values. A ``multiple`` argument always resolves to a list: when it is "
"omitted it resolves to an empty list, or — if a ``default`` is configured — "
"to a single-item list containing that default. Supplying values on the "
"command line replaces the default rather than adding to it. In a cmd task "
"the values can be passed as separate arguments to the task via templating as "
"in the following example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:193
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.save] cmd  = \"echo ${FILE_PATHS}\" args = [{ name = "
"\"FILE_PATHS\", positional = true, multiple = true }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:197
msgid "**choices** : ``list[str | int | float]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:196
msgid "list[str | int | float]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:198
msgid ""
"Constrain the accepted values for an argument to a fixed set. The choices "
"are shown in task help output. For non-string argument types, the choices "
"must be specified using the same type (e.g. ``integer`` choices should be "
"numbers). This option is not compatible with ``type = \"boolean\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:200
msgid "**required** : ``bool``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:201
msgid ""
"If true then not providing the argument will result in an error. Arguments "
"are not required by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:203
msgid "**type** : ``Literal[\"string\", \"float\", \"integer\", \"boolean\"]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:202
msgid "Literal[\"string\", \"float\", \"integer\", \"boolean\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:204
msgid ""
"The type that the provided value will be cast to. If not provided then the "
"default behaviour is to keep values as strings. Setting the type to "
"``\"boolean\"`` makes the resulting argument a flag that if provided will "
"set the value to the boolean opposite of the default value – i.e. :toml:"
"`true` if no default value is given, or :toml:`false` if :toml:`default = "
"true`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:206
msgid ""
"When ``type = \"boolean\"``, the ``default`` option (if set) must be a TOML "
"boolean, or one of the following case-insensitive string literals (also "
"accepting surrounding whitespace): ``\"t\"``, ``\"true\"``, ``\"1\"`` for "
"true, and ``\"f\"``, ``\"false\"``, ``\"0\"``, ``\"\"`` for false. A "
"templated string (e.g. ``\"${SOME_VAR}\"``) is also accepted and re-checked "
"against this same set once the template has been resolved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:210
msgid ""
"When a :toml:`type = \"boolean\"` flag arg evaluates to :toml:`false`, this "
"results in the corresponding environment variable (if any) being *unset* for "
"the task, even if it was previously set by some other means. This ensures "
"consistent *non-truthy* semantics for :ref:`parameter expansion "
"operators<Parameter expansion operators>`, in config, shells or other "
"subprocesses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:212
msgid ""
"If the flag value is :toml:`true` then it is exposed as a python variable "
"with the value ``True`` in expr or script tasks, and an environment variable "
"with the string value ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:216
msgid "Constraining argument values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:218
msgid ""
"You can document and enforce that only specific values should be accepted "
"for an argument by specifying the ``choices`` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:220
msgid ""
" [tool.poe.tasks.share]\n"
" help = \"Share some personal information\"\n"
" cmd = \"echo \\\"My favorite ice cream is ${flavor}\\\"\"\n"
"[[tool.poe.tasks.share.args]]\n"
"name = \"flavor\"\n"
"help = \"Favorite ice cream\"\n"
"positional = true\n"
"choices = [\"chocolate\", \"pistachio\", \"vanilla\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:231
msgid "The resulting help output includes the choices:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:233
msgid ""
"$ poe --help share\n"
"\n"
"Description:\n"
"  Run Share some personal information\n"
"\n"
"Usage:\n"
"  poe [global options] check [named arguments] -- [free arguments]\n"
"\n"
"Named arguments:\n"
"  flavor               Favorite ice cream [choices: 'chocolate', "
"'pistachio', 'vanilla']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:247
msgid "Arguments for cmd and shell tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:249
msgid ""
"For :doc:`cmd<../tasks/task_types/cmd>` and :doc:`shell<../tasks/task_types/"
"shell>` tasks the values are exposed to the task as environment variables. "
"For example given the following configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:251
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.passby]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"\n"
"echo \"hello $planet\";\n"
"echo \"goodbye $planet\";\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"help = \"Pass by a planet!\"\n"
"\n"
"  [[tool.poe.tasks.passby.args]]\n"
"  name = \"planet\"\n"
"  help = \"Name of the planet to pass\"\n"
"  default = \"earth\"\n"
"  options = [\"-p\", \"--planet\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:266
msgid "The resulting task can be run like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:268
msgid "poe passby --planet mars"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:273
msgid ""
"For PowerShell tasks, the variable needs to be referenced as an environment "
"variable in the shell code, e.g., :code:`$env:planet`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:276
msgid "Arguments for script tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:278
msgid ""
"Arguments can be defined for :doc:`script<../tasks/task_types/script>` tasks "
"in the same way, but how they are exposed to the underlying Python function "
"depends on how the script is defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:280
msgid ""
"In the following example, since no parenthesis are included for the "
"referenced function, all provided args will be passed to the function as "
"keyword arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:282
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.build]\n"
"script = \"my_app.util:build\"\n"
"args = [\"dest\", \"version\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:288
msgid ""
"You can also control exactly how values are passed to the Python function as "
"demonstrated in the following example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:290
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.build]\n"
"script = \"my_app.util:build(_dest, build_version=_version, verbose=True)\"\n"
"args = [\n"
"  { name = \"_dest\" },\n"
"  { name = \"_version\", type = \"integer\" }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:299
msgid ""
"In the second example, the arg names are prefixed with an underscore, which "
"combined with the fact that they're all lowercase makes them private, i.e. "
"they are not propagated to environment variables on the task subprocess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:301
msgid ""
"Note also that because the type of _version is integer, the value passed to "
"the ``--version`` CLI argument will be parsed to an int and passed as such "
"when calling the build function in python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:304
msgid "Arguments for sequence tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:306
msgid ""
"Arguments can be passed to the tasks referenced from a sequence task as in "
"the following example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:309
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.build]\n"
"script = \"util:build_app\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"target\", positional = true }]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.check]\n"
"sequence = [\"build ${_target}\", { script = \"util:run_tests(_target)\" }]\n"
"args = [\"_target\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:319
msgid ""
"This works by setting the argument values as either private or environment "
"variables for the subtasks, which can be referenced in the task definition "
"as demonstrated in the above example for a :doc:`ref<../tasks/task_types/"
"ref>` task and :doc:`script<../tasks/task_types/script>` task. Non-private "
"variables can be accessed as environment variables at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:321
msgid ""
"Notice that the argument is accessible like a python variable in the script "
"subtask."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:324
msgid "Passing free arguments in addition to named arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:326
msgid ""
"If no args are defined for a cmd task then any cli arguments that are "
"provided are simply appended to the command. If named arguments are defined "
"then one can still provide additional free arguments to the command by "
"separating them from the defined arguments with a double dash token :sh:`--`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:328
msgid "For example given a task like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:330
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.lint]\n"
"cmd  = \"ruff check ${target_dir}\"\n"
"args = { target_dir = { options = [\"--target\", \"-t\"], default = \".\" }}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:336
msgid "calling the task like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:338
msgid "poe lint -t tests -- --fix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:342
msgid ""
"will result in poe parsing the target_dir cli option, but appending the :sh:"
"`--fix` flag to the ruff command without attempting to interpret it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:346
msgid ""
"Passing :sh:`--` in the arguments list to any other task type will simply "
"result in any subsequent arguments being ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:352
msgid "Forwarding free arguments via ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:354
msgid ""
"When a task receives free arguments (i.e. arguments not matched by any named "
"arg definition, or arguments passed after :sh:`--`), poe sets a special "
"environment variable ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` containing those arguments as a "
"shell-quoted, space-delimited string. This variable is inherited by all "
"subtasks and can be referenced explicitly wherever an environment variable "
"can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:356
msgid ""
"``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` can be used in any task type that supports environment "
"variable expansion: :doc:`cmd<../tasks/task_types/cmd>`, :doc:`ref<../tasks/"
"task_types/ref>`, :doc:`shell<../tasks/task_types/shell>`. In :doc:"
"`script<../tasks/task_types/script>` and :doc:`expr<../tasks/task_types/"
"expr>` tasks free arguments are also accessible as the ``_extra_args`` "
"variable (a ``list[str]``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:360
msgid "Sequence, parallel and switch tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:362
msgid ""
"Free arguments passed to a :doc:`sequence<../tasks/task_types/sequence>`, :"
"doc:`parallel<../tasks/task_types/parallel>`, or :doc:`switch<../tasks/"
"task_types/switch>` task are not forwarded to subtasks automatically. By "
"referencing ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` in individual subtask definitions you can "
"choose which subtasks in the group receive the extra arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:364
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-py39]\n"
"cmd = \"pytest tests\"\n"
"executor = { type = \"uv\", isolated = true, python = \"3.9\" }\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-py310]\n"
"cmd = \"pytest tests\"\n"
"executor = { type = \"uv\", isolated = true, python = \"3.10\" }\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.lint]\n"
"cmd = \"ruff check .\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-all]\n"
"sequence = [\"test-py39 $POE_EXTRA_ARGS\", \"test-py310 $POE_EXTRA_ARGS\", "
"\"lint\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:380
msgid "Calling this like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:382
msgid "poe test-all --cov=mypackage -v"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:386
msgid ""
"forwards ``--cov=mypackage -v`` to ``test-py39`` and ``test-py310``, but not "
"to ``lint``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:388
msgid ""
"``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` can appear anywhere in the subtask command string — "
"including between other arguments — so it is possible to add fixed options "
"before *and* after the forwarded arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:390
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-all]\n"
"sequence = [\n"
"  \"test-py39 --timeout=30 $POE_EXTRA_ARGS --tb=short\",\n"
"  \"test-py310 --timeout=30 $POE_EXTRA_ARGS --tb=short\",\n"
"  \"lint\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:401
msgid "Cmd tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:403
msgid ""
"For :doc:`cmd<../tasks/task_types/cmd>` tasks, free arguments are appended "
"to the end of the command by default. By referencing ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` "
"explicitly in the command string, poe expands it in place and does *not* "
"additionally append the extra args at the end. This is useful when fixed "
"options must follow the free arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:405
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.coverage]\n"
"cmd = \"pytest $POE_EXTRA_ARGS --cov=mypackage\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:410
msgid ""
"Calling :sh:`poe coverage tests/unit -x` produces ``pytest tests/unit -x --"
"cov=mypackage`` instead of ``pytest --cov=mypackage tests/unit -x``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:414
msgid "Script and expr tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:416
msgid ""
"In :doc:`script<../tasks/task_types/script>` tasks free arguments are "
"available as the ``_extra_args`` variable (a ``list[str]``) that can be "
"referenced directly in the function call expression:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:418
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.run-tests]\n"
"script = \"pytest:main(_extra_args)\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:423
msgid ""
"Calling :sh:`poe run-tests tests/unit -x -v` will call ``pytest."
"main([\"tests/unit\", \"-x\", \"-v\"])``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:425
msgid ""
":doc:`Expr<../tasks/task_types/expr>` tasks work the same way — "
"``_extra_args`` is available as a Python variable inside the expression:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/args_guide.rst:427
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.count-args]\n"
"expr = \"len(_extra_args)\""
msgstr ""
